      {
        "id": "WE_B8_MOSQ_01",
        "questionPrompt": "A junior high school student captured two different adult mosquitoes, Specimen A and Specimen B, resting on a bedroom wall at dusk.\n- Specimen A rested with its body tilted outward at an acute angle of 45°, its wings showed distinct dark and pale scale clusters, and its maxillary palps were exactly the same length as its proboscis.\n- Specimen B rested with its body held parallel to the wall, had completely plain brownish wings, and possessed very short maxillary palps.\n(a) Identify the genus of Specimen A and Specimen B.\n(b) State the major human disease transmitted by each specimen.\n(c) Describe two diagnostic differences between the aquatic larvae of Specimen A and Specimen B.",
        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: Identify Specimen A and Specimen B — Specimen A is an Anopheles mosquito because it exhibits the classic 45° resting angle, palps equal in length to the proboscis, and spotted wings. Specimen B is a Culex mosquito because it rests parallel to the surface, has plain wings, and features short maxillary palps.\nStep 2: State the Transmitted Diseases — Specimen A (Anopheles) transmits Malaria (caused by Plasmodium protozoa). Specimen B (Culex) transmits Lymphatic Filariasis / Elephantiasis (caused by Wuchereria bancrofti nematode worms).\nStep 3: Contrast the Aquatic Larval Stages — (1) Posture at water surface: Anopheles larvae float completely horizontal and parallel to the water surface, whereas Culex larvae hang suspended downward at an angle. (2) Breathing organ: Anopheles larvae completely lack a breathing siphon (breathing through abdominal spiracles), whereas Culex larvae possess a long, prominent tubular breathing siphon.",
        "examinerTip": "Examiner Tip: In BECE practical tests, always memorize the 'Rule of 45': Anopheles adults rest at 45° while their larvae rest at 0° (parallel); Culex adults rest at 0° (parallel) while their larvae hang at an angle!"
      },
      {
        "id": "WE_B8_MOSQ_02",
        "questionPrompt": "A student was bitten by an infected female Anopheles mosquito while studying outdoors at night. Twelve days later, the student developed severe chills, shaking, high fever, and extreme fatigue.\n(a) Name the infectious stage of the parasite injected by the mosquito.\n(b) Explain why symptoms did not appear immediately after the bite (the 12-day delay).\n(c) What specific cellular event in the bloodstream triggers the violent shaking chills and high fever?",
        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: Name the Injected Parasite Stage — The infectious stage injected through the mosquito's salivary gland is the sporozoite (motile, spindle-shaped stage of Plasmodium).\nStep 2: Explain the Incubation Period Delay — The 12-day delay represents the biological incubation period. Injected sporozoites do not immediately attack red blood cells; they first enter liver parenchymal cells (hepatic phase). Inside liver cells, they undergo extensive asexual multiplication (schizogony) for 7 to 10 days to produce thousands of merozoites before invading the bloodstream.\nStep 3: Explain the Cellular Trigger for Fever and Chills — Once in the blood, merozoites multiply synchronously inside red blood cells (erythrocytic phase). When these parasitized erythrocytes simultaneously burst open, they dump thousands of new merozoites, cellular debris, and toxic metabolic byproducts (hemozoin pigments) directly into the blood plasma. These pyrogenic toxins stimulate host white blood cells to release cytokines that reset the hypothalamus thermostat, causing abrupt shivering chills and fever spikes.",
        "examinerTip": "Examiner Tip: Remember the sequence of parasite forms: Sporozoite (injected by mosquito) → Liver cell multiplication → Merozoite (invades and ruptures RBCs) → Gametocyte (taken up by mosquito)."
      },
      {
        "id": "WE_B8_MOSQ_03",
        "questionPrompt": "An environmental health officer poured a thin film of kerosene over a stagnant breeding ditch containing hundreds of active mosquito larvae ('wrigglers') and pupae ('tumblers'). Within 30 minutes, all wrigglers and tumblers were dead at the bottom of the ditch.\n(a) Explain the biophysical mechanism by which the kerosene film killed both larvae and pupae.\n(b) Why are adult mosquitoes unaffected by surface kerosene films?",
        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: Explain the Physical Suffocation of Larvae and Pupae — Both mosquito larvae and pupae are obligate air-breathers that must repeatedly penetrate the water surface film to draw atmospheric oxygen. When kerosene is poured, it spreads into an unbroken monolayer that drastically lowers the surface tension of water. (1) When larvae attempt to breathe, the oily liquid enters and clogs their eighth abdominal spiracles. (2) When pupae rise, kerosene enters their respiratory trumpets. Blocked from atmospheric oxygen, both stages rapidly asphyxiate (suffocate) and sink to the bottom.\nStep 2: Explain Why Adults are Unaffected — Adult mosquitoes are fully aerial terrestrial insects with wings and legs. They do not live submerged in water, nor do they breathe through aquatic spiracles or trumpets. They fly in the air and intake oxygen through thoracic and abdominal spiracles surrounded by hydrophobic scales, keeping them physically detached from surface water films.",
        "examinerTip": "Examiner Tip: When explaining the action of oil/kerosene, emphasize that it is a physical control method that causes suffocation/asphyxiation by blocking breathing pores, rather than a chemical stomach poison."
      },
      {
        "id": "WE_B8_MOSQ_04",
        "questionPrompt": "A junior high school located near a marshy valley experiences high rates of pupil absenteeism due to recurrent malaria cases. The Headteacher proposes purchasing chemical aerosol sprays to fog all classrooms every morning.\n(a) Identify two severe scientific drawbacks of relying exclusively on daily chemical indoor fogging.\n(b) Formulate an alternative 4-pillar Integrated Pest Management (IPM) plan to permanently suppress the mosquito population around the school.",
        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: Critique Exclusive Chemical Fogging — (1) Chemical Resistance: Continuous exposure to aerosol chemicals creates strong selective pressure, causing surviving mosquitoes to develop pesticide resistance via enzyme mutations, rendering sprays ineffective. (2) Environmental & Health Toxicity: Daily chemical fumes cause respiratory irritation (asthma, coughing) among pupils, contaminate classroom surfaces, and kill beneficial pollinating insects (bees, butterflies).\nStep 2: Propose the 4-Pillar IPM Plan —\n- Pillar 1: Environmental Control (Source Reduction): Organize a weekly school clean-up to desilt blocked gutters, drain waterlogged depressions, clear tall grasses around school buildings, and bury empty tins, coconut shells, and plastic containers.\n- Pillar 2: Physical Barriers: Install durable wire mesh screens on all classroom and dormitory windows and doors, and ensure all boarding pupils sleep under Long-Lasting Insecticide-Treated Nets (LLINs).\n- Pillar 3: Biological Control: Introduce surface-feeding larvivorous fish (Gambusia affinis or juvenile Tilapia) into nearby marshy irrigation ponds to continuously consume larvae, or apply microbial Bacillus thuringiensis israelensis (BTI) larvicide.\n- Pillar 4: Targeted Chemical Control: Apply periodic Indoor Residual Spraying (IRS) on dormitory walls once every six months using approved, rotating chemical formulations to avoid resistance, and apply eco-friendly oil films to non-drainable stagnant puddles.",
        "examinerTip": "Examiner Tip: High-scoring IPM answers must explicitly cover all four categories: Environmental (breeding elimination), Physical (barriers/nets), Biological (natural predators/bacteria), and Chemical (targeted residual sprays)."
      }
