      {
        "id": "ex_b7_s10_1",
        "questionPrompt": "A smallholder farmer in the Northern Region of Ghana cultivates maize and cowpea. When grown as sole crops, 1.0 hectare produces 4.0 metric tonnes (t/ha) of maize and 1.5 metric tonnes of cowpea. When intercropped simultaneously on the same 1.0 hectare plot, the harvested yields are 3.0 t/ha of maize and 0.9 t/ha of cowpea.\n\n(a) Calculate the Land Equivalent Ratio (LER) for this maize-cowpea intercropping enterprise.\n(b) Explain whether the farmer achieved an agronomic yield advantage by intercropping, and state the biological reason for this outcome.",
        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: State the standard formula for Land Equivalent Ratio (LER):\nLER = (Yield of Crop 1 in Intercrop / Yield of Crop 1 in Sole Crop) + (Yield of Crop 2 in Intercrop / Yield of Crop 2 in Sole Crop)\nStep 2: Substitute the empirical yield figures into the formula:\nPartial LER for Maize = 3.0 / 4.0 = 0.75\nPartial LER for Cowpea = 0.9 / 1.5 = 0.60\nCombined LER = 0.75 + 0.60 = 1.35\nStep 3: Agronomic interpretation of the calculated LER:\nAn LER of 1.35 is strictly greater than 1.0 (LER > 1.0). This demonstrates a 35% yield advantage from intercropping. It signifies that a sole-crop farmer would require 1.35 hectares of land (0.75 ha of sole maize + 0.60 ha of sole cowpea) to produce the exact same harvest that the intercropper generated from just 1.0 hectare.\nStep 4: Biological and environmental justification:\nThe yield advantage occurs due to resource complementarity and minimized interspecific niche competition: tall maize intercepts high-angle sunlight while deep-rooted cowpea intercepts low-angle light and fixes atmospheric nitrogen via root nodule Rhizobium bacteria, enriching available soil nitrogen pools.",
        "examinerTip": "Examiner Tip: (a) LER = 1.35. (b) The farmer achieved a 35% yield advantage over sole cropping due to complementary canopy solar radiation capture and biological nitrogen fixation."
      },
      {
        "id": "ex_b7_s10_2",
        "questionPrompt": "A JHS agricultural science club is designing a 3-bed crop rotation scheme using Tomato, Cowpea, and Carrot. Concurrently, the farm enterprise accountant compiles the club's financial balance sheet showing total farm assets of GHS 85,000 and total liabilities of GHS 32,000.\n\n(a) Design the rotational matrix for Beds 1, 2, and 3 across Years 1, 2, and 3, justifying the sequence based on feeding habits and botanical families.\n(b) Calculate the club's Net Worth (Owner's Equity) and evaluate whether the farm is financially solvent.",
        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: Construct the 3-Year / 3-Bed Rotational Matrix adhering to agronomic rules:\nBed 1: Year 1 = Tomato (Heavy Feeder / Solanaceae) → Year 2 = Cowpea (N-Fixer / Fabaceae) → Year 3 = Carrot (Light Feeder / Apiaceae)\nBed 2: Year 1 = Cowpea (N-Fixer / Fabaceae) → Year 2 = Carrot (Light Feeder / Apiaceae) → Year 3 = Tomato (Heavy Feeder / Solanaceae)\nBed 3: Year 1 = Carrot (Light Feeder / Apiaceae) → Year 2 = Tomato (Heavy Feeder / Solanaceae) → Year 3 = Cowpea (N-Fixer / Fabaceae)\nStep 2: Justify the rotational sequence:\n1. Botanical Family Alternation: Solanaceae, Fabaceae, and Apiaceae never follow themselves on the same bed, starving family-specific pests and soil pathogens.\n2. Root Depth & Nutrient Alternation: Heavy feeder Tomato depletes topsoil NPK; restorative Cowpea nodulates with Rhizobium to fix N₂; deep tap-rooted Carrot draws residual minerals from subsoil while aerating the profile.\nStep 3: Calculate the financial Net Worth (Owner's Equity):\nNet Worth = Total Assets - Total Liabilities\nNet Worth = GHS 85,000 - GHS 32,000 = GHS 53,000\nStep 4: Evaluate financial solvency:\nBecause Total Assets (GHS 85,000) exceed Total Liabilities (GHS 32,000), Owner's Equity is positive (+GHS 53,000). The enterprise is economically solvent and possesses positive long-term capital stability.",
        "examinerTip": "Examiner Tip: Net Worth = GHS 53,000 (Solvent). In the rotation, always sequence: Heavy Feeder → Leguminous Restorer → Light/Root Feeder."
      }
