        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: Construct the comparative matrix —\n- Definition: Heat is a form of energy in transit equal to the total internal kinetic and potential energy of all particles in a body; Temperature is a measure of the degree of hotness or coldness (proportional to average kinetic energy per particle).\n- SI Unit: Heat is measured in Joules (J); Temperature is measured in Kelvin (K) [metric: °C].\n- Instrument: Heat is measured using a calorimeter; Temperature is measured using a thermometer.\n- Mass Dependence: Heat is an extensive property (proportional to mass); Temperature is an intensive property (independent of sample mass).\nStep 2: Evaluate higher temperature —\nThe small test tube has the higher temperature (100°C vs. 40°C). Temperature measures the average kinetic energy of the individual water molecules; molecules at 100°C vibrate and translate significantly faster than molecules at 40°C.\nStep 3: Evaluate greater total heat content —\nThe 200-liter drum contains far more total heat energy. Heat is an extensive property dependent on the total number of particles. Even though each molecule in the drum has a lower average speed, the drum holds 20,000 times more molecules (200,000 ml vs. 10 ml), resulting in a vastly greater cumulative internal energy sum.",
