        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: State three physical effects of heat —\n1. Rise in Temperature: Absorbing thermal energy accelerates particle vibration, elevating temperature.\n2. Thermal Expansion: Heating causes particles to vibrate more energetically and move farther apart, increasing length, surface area, and volume.\n3. Change of State (Phase Transition): Thermal energy converts solids to liquids (melting) or liquids to gases (vaporization/boiling).\nStep 2: Explain constant temperature during melting (Latent Heat of Fusion) —\nDuring melting, all the absorbed thermal energy is consumed as latent heat of fusion. This energy is strictly expended in overcoming and breaking the rigid intermolecular hydrogen bonds holding water molecules in their fixed crystalline ice lattice, rather than increasing the translational kinetic energy (speed) of the molecules. Because temperature is strictly a measure of average molecular kinetic energy, the temperature cannot rise until the crystal lattice is completely broken and all ice has transitioned into liquid water.",
