        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: Evaluate force equilibrium:\nForward applied force (F_applied) = +175 N (to the right).\nOpposing frictional force (F_friction) = -75 N (to the left).\nBecause the magnitudes are unequal (+175 N ≠ -75 N), the net force is non-zero. The horizontal forces are UNBALANCED.\nStep 2: Calculate the net horizontal force (F_net):\nF_net = F_applied - F_friction = 175 N - 75 N = +100 N (directed to the right).\nStep 3: Calculate acceleration using Newton's Second Law (F_net = m × a):\nState the formula: a = F_net / m\nSubstitute values: a = 100 N / 50 kg = 2.0 m/s² (to the right).\nStep 4: Analyze the condition when F_applied is reduced to 75 N:\nNew net force: F_net = 75 N - 75 N = 0 N.\nThe forces are now BALANCED. According to Newton's First Law of Motion, an object experiencing zero net force does NOT stop instantaneously; it ceases accelerating and continues moving forward at a CONSTANT VELOCITY in a straight line until an external unbalanced force intervenes.",
        "examinerTip": "Examiner Tip: (a) Unbalanced forces; (b) Net force = 100 N to the right; (c) Acceleration = 2.0 m/s²; (d) When net force becomes 0 N, the crate maintains uniform forward motion at constant velocity (Newton's First Law)."
